welcome everyone to seafarer's series of  ritual  talks and today's conversation with  steven reichert  these online talks are intended to  showcase how research  conducted by seafarers fellows can help  inform decision making  as governments develop policies to  reopen shuttered societies  and economies in the wake of covid19  if you're not familiar with us cfar is a  canadian-based global research  organization  that convenes extraordinary minds from  around the world to address some of the  most important questions facing science  and humanity  like many other organizations cfar has  pivoted quickly to take action on covet  19  launching new projects and initiatives  some of which were highlighting through  this series my name is kate getty  i'm a senior director of research at  seafar and it's my pleasure to introduce  you to  professor steven reichert today steve  is a professor of psychology at the  university of saint andrews  and a leading authority on social  social psychology his work focuses on  questions of social identity  collective behavior crowd behavior and  leadership  and he is a fellow in seafarer's new  boundaries membership and belonging  research program  in recent months steve has served on  behavioral science advisory groups  to both the scottish and uk governments  concerning their responses to the kovit  19 pandemic  and despite how busy that must have kept  him he  has amazingly managed to co-author a  book that was published in late may  titled together apart  the psychology of covid19 given the  intense interest in this topic then and  now  it was published at that time online as  an uncorrected proof  the premise of the book is that unless  or until  a vaccine for the coronavirus is  developed our means of controlling the  spread of infection  depend on behavioral changes and hence  upon human psychology  so in today's webinar steve will share  with us insights from this book  that uses social identity theorizing to  analyze the psychology of the coded 19  pandemic  such as what encourages us to come  together and support one another as a  community to help us through the crisis  before we dive in i'll quickly set the  parameters  for today's session which will last 30  minutes  i'll take my advantage of a position as  host to ask steve the first few  questions before opening it up to  questions from you  the online participants so if you have a  question  please submit it through the q a  function at the bottom of your screen  and lastly i'll mention that the session  is being recorded  and will be available on the cfart  website next week so  welcome steve thanks very much  to start our conversation what is the  field of social psychology  and why is it particularly important to  understand identity and group processes  under the current pandemic okay well let  me start  well i'm not sure whether i'm correcting  you or correcting myself because you  were citing something that i said about  um behavior being at the center of this  until we get a vaccine  actually it's going to be at the center  of this even when we have a vaccine  because a vaccine does not stop the  infection  people getting vaccinated does and  people are going to therefore be willing  to be vaccinated and  the latest polling evidence i saw showed  that um  something like a third of people are  saying they wouldn't take such a vaccine  so there's a huge behavioral question  there  or equally if we discuss masks say  there's a lot of discussion  do masks stop the infection well masks  stop nothing  um wearing a mask might and again we're  talking about behaviors we're talking  about whether people  wear them uh how they wear them how  effectively they wear them  and most obviously with with lockdown  lock down uh as a policy does nothing  unless people agree to be locked down so  right at the core  of every intervention we have including  when we have a vaccine  is dependent upon behavior and human  behavior  now through this pandemic there have  been two  very different approaches to human  behavior and two ways of thinking about  uh the place of the public in the  pandemic response  one is a very traditional view and it's  a rather elitist view  and it's a view that has at its core a  notion that human beings  are essentially psychologically frail we  have a  tenuous grasp on reality we are full of  biases  um we have a tenuous uh  moral compass which is easily thrown  aside  and that fragility is  like exacerbated under conditions a  where we're in the group the group still  further  undermines our rationality and secondly  when there's a crisis  and that's exemplified by the notion of  panic the notion is  that in a crisis people panic a  hollywood disaster film would never be  complete without a scene of people  running away  screaming waving their hands in the air  and clogging up the exits so it's human  psychology and the frailty  of the human subject which turns  a crisis into a tragedy and therefore a  public are a real problem  now when you look more closely at  crises actually that's not what you find  what you find on the whole is actually  people behave in a fairly restrained way  they support each other they don't just  look after themselves  and they are rather resilient when it  happens every  nation thinks that there's something  special about us because we behaved in a  restraint and a supportive way  um so in britain we like to think it's  the blitz spirit that's what does it  we're different from the rest of you  uh if you go to new york they'll tell  you it's the new york spirit you saw it  in 9 11.  actually it happens nearly everywhere  the reason why it happens  is that when we have common fate  when we face a common predicament  what we see is the emergence of a shared  identity  a sense of us and that sense of us that  extension of the self to include  others has a number of consequences  including support for others  coordination with others and therefore  with  others working with others when others  become an asset  to us rather than the problem actually  we become psychologically resilient  we become empowered and we become rather  effective and what we've seen  in this pandemic in many countries  certainly we saw it in the uk i think  you saw it in  canada as well is that when that shared  sense of identity  is understood when it is nurtured by  leadership when it is scaffolded by  policies which bring people together  then  actually the public uh develop a sense  of collective resilience and that sense  of collective resilience is in many ways  the best asset you have in a pandemic  and so just to finish this comment with  for me if i had a top 10 quotes of the  pandemic  i'm sure there will be lists like that  in in sunday magazines and years to come  um my number one would be andrew cuomo  the governor of new york who said  it's not about i it's about we get your  head  around the wii concept and what my work  has tried to do for many years is to try  and understand  the we concept i call it social identity  concept will  uh do and understand how it can be  developed  and harnessed and how the group becomes  the asset rather than the problem in a  pandemic  that's interesting well i'd like to pick  up on the theme that you  mentioned around the role of political  leaders and suggesting that they have a  critical role  in fostering necessary group behaviors  and dealing with a health emergency  what do we know about what's needed in  terms of effective political leadership  in an emergency situation like pandemic  well let me start with the concept of  leadership itself  um and since since since you plugged the  the together apart book also this week  we did we uh  released with with my colleague alex  haslam and michael plathor a book on  leadership  so leadership is something that  interests me and  when you look at traditional notions of  leadership  they are again very hierarchical and  very  elitist and they start from the premise  that what makes a great leader is what  sets them apart from the rest of us you  know the great leader has these amazing  qualities  uh and if only you could understand  what it was if you could isolate this  right stuff and bottle it you would have  solved the problem of leadership  um now the problem with that well it has  many problems first of all it ignores  the fact that  leaders again don't achieve anything on  their own  they literally they achieve it through  followers so uh  leadership which ignores followers is is  an  empty model empirically i mean that  notion of of  uh it is highly gendered great man  leadership has been around for a long  time  um thomas carlisle famously in uh i  think was about 1848 his book on heroes  and hero worship said that  uh history is effectively uh  what great men have done while they were  uh  on earth um thousands of studies trying  to  find empirically what is the quality  that shows leadership  um and they've all failed and they  failed because actually they missed the  point that what makes a great leader  is what actually brings them together  with  the group they seek to lead  what makes them able to represent that  group speak  for that group shape the understanding  of the group  so what the qualities that you need in a  leader  aren't abstract or absolute it's more a  matter of a  fit between our understanding of who we  are and  the leaders definition of who they are  and leaders um we call them  entrepreneurs of identity are very  skilled at telling a narrative of the  group  and a narrative of themselves which  fleshes those two together  and particularly what you need in a  pandemic is you need a sense of  shared identity you need to nurture that  sense of  us-ness you need to bring people  together to  support each other um and actually to  to do the practical things but also to  provide the psychological support  to take us through now if one of the  criteria  for being an effective leader is to  create a unified  group then we see huge differences  between different leaders and different  places um  i think the leaders we've seen who are  effective um  uh so for instance justin ardern perhaps  in new zealand  um luckily enough i don't live in  england i live in scotland so we've seen  it with nicolas sturgeon  actually it's quite interesting when you  when you name the leaders you see they  have something in common  um they tend to be women um but these  are leaders  who have been very effective in  nurturing  uh a sense of us and in representing  that sense  of us and also of course in developing  the policies  which support the collective  self-interest  but again let me make a point here it's  a simple point but actually it's a  rather consequential point  in so much of our social science we  start from the premise  that individuals act in terms of  individual self-interest  and the way to motivate people is to  tell them what is in their individual  self-interest  the problem with that again is it  equates the self with the individual  it ignores the fact that we can define  ourselves in collective  terms and it ignores the fact that  actually in the pandemic  what is important is we don't act for  ourselves as individuals  but we act for the community so  very simply if people acting in terms of  individual self-interest the hell of a  lot of people in this pandemic  would save themselves actually if i do  the cost benefit analysis  i lose more by staying in them by going  out if you're young and you're healthy  actually you don't have much to risk uh  by going out and you lose a hell of a  lot by staying in i have a  16 year old next door who is not very  happy about having to stay in he suffers  far more than i do  so on the basis of individual  self-interest your calculation would say  go out  but of course at a collective level if  you go out  you spread the disease other people get  ill and  die so if you want to contain the  pandemic people need to be  thinking of themselves in terms of that  collective identity  and by and large that's what happened so  for instance i know  the uk data there may well be data in  canada as well but it shows  that actually the main factor which  determined whether people obeyed  lockdown or not  was not their sense of individual risk  it was their sense of communal risk  their sense of community identity so  again  understanding the we concept creating a  sense of  us and representing that sense of us is  absolutely central to leadership  however and actually here um uh i should  i i could try and show how much i know  about canada  but i know nothing about canada but  luckily yeah uh kate you you you  mean yeah i read about bonnie henry  and i loved her quotation about um we're  all in the same  uh storm we're not in the same boat and  the problem is it's all very well  to talk the talk of shared identity  and to talk the talk of an inclusive  sense of us-ness  but of course different people within  the national community are in very  different positions  and are affected very differently  um by the pandemic it's opened up and  shown a spot by tomorrow  all that all our inequalities um and  therefore  part of creating a shared identity is  not only to do it rhetorically but to do  it practically the practical economic  measures of for instance  supporting people so they could afford  to stay at home  giving uh money for those who are  unemployed giving  uh money for those who are self-employed  and and so on and only if in practice  you create the conditions for a shared  experience and a  shared response will you be able to  create that shared identity so as i say  it's a matter  of rhetoric but it's also a matter of  practical policy  uh which means that we're not only in  the same storm but we are  at least to some extent in the same boat  well now that it's been almost four  months at least  in canada since the major covet 19  restrictions were put in place  we're hearing more and more um reference  to things like quarantine fatigue  is there evidence that there are  potential limits to our psychological  ability to put up with restrictions  you see this was a huge controversy in  the uk and before we locked down  we had people talking about behavioral  fatigue uh  and they they actually used it to uh  justify  a delay uh in lockdown in the uk and  that  arguably that could have cost tens of  thousands of lives i mean  um uh depending on how you calculate it  i mean you've lost about eight thousand  lives from what i can see in canada  we've lost about sixty thousand we've  done pretty badly  um and this behave this notion of  behavioral fatigue if  it um uh led to a delay it  it's a big issue okay now  when you look at the notion of fatigue  it is perfectly  true that uh it is difficult to sustain  lockdown over a prolonged period so the  fact that there is fatigue and  compliance  decreases i think is not controversial  what is controversial is what is the  root of that fatigue  is it an essential psychological  fragility and that takes me back to  what i was saying about different  psychological models or is it something  else  so let me give you just one i think very  striking piece of evidence again i  apologize  it's british evidence but i suspect it's  the same in different places  in the uk you found a difference of a  factor of  six between the most affluent and the  least affluent in how often they went  out under under lockdown  okay there's a huge difference in  compliance  but when you looked at motivation when  you looked at the psychological will  to stay in there was no difference  between those groups at all  the difference lay in just basically the  ability to stay at home  and um uh put food on the table  so there are a couple of factors which  are absolutely critical  in determining your ability to go along  with something  one is of course the information you're  given  people might suffer for a cause but if  there's no cause to suffer for people  aren't going to suffer for it so  if the messaging is bad and if the  messaging suggests to you oh well  actually it's all over  there's no danger then people won't  comply so  failures of government messaging we've  seen that in the uk  undermine compliance and the second  thing is practical if people can't  afford  um to stay at home and feed their  families they won't do so  so to my mind the evidence for the  psychological fragility is actually  rather weak people showed  incredible resilience under lockdown i  mean  again in the uk the figures showed that  something like 44  of people were suffering but they still  stayed in i think this has  undermined those notions of fragility  and showed  collective resilience when people work  together the problems as i say have been  those of  messaging and our government has been  very bad at this  and of giving practical support and so  to talk about  fatigue being a psychological problem in  many ways for me  is the government blaming its own  failures on the failure of human  psychology  and i think that's a very dangerous uh  as well as a very unfair thing to do  so as i mentioned at the outset you've  been advising  both the scottish and uk governments on  how to encourage adherence to public  health behaviors  in a previous c4 webinar we had one of  our other fellows tim besley who's part  of our institutions organizations and  growth program who spoke  to the government use of research  evidence for policy making  this seems to be a particularly  political issue  in the uk right now can you tell us  why and how you feel government should  use insights from  academic research both to guide the  pandemic response but also  a post-pandemic recovery so i think the  responsibility  responsibility is always round and  i mean academics have got to be open  about the limits of what we know and the  certainties of our own  evidence and clearly given that  uh covert 19 is new and we're learning  things about it  all the time uh both in epidemiological  terms but also in psychological terms  there are certain things we couldn't  know yet we have some general principles  that we could apply um and there i think  one of the benefits of  of psychological research is that on the  whole we do try and understand  if you like general processes of  behavior so i can  speak for instance to the role of  social identities and social norms in  behavior  and then i can apply that to the  particular types  of identities you've drawn or the  particular types of norms  which is necessary to reconfigure in  order to get behavior so i think  i think we have to be as i say open  about our own limitations  and then from the perspective of  government i think there are two  pitfalls  one being to ignore the science  because the science of course can tell  you what the consequence of particular  actions will be  but the other error and i think it's  equally problematic  is to claim to be led by the science  because if the science can tell you  what particular outcomes are likely to  be what it can't do  is tell you you know how much risk  should you or shouldn't you take  or how do you balance different risks  against each other  because that's a political question or a  value question  if you like and to claim that you are  led by the science can sometimes  hide the political choices which i think  is dangerous and disingenuous  so i think it's very important to get  the balance right and to say that  government should listen to us  and governments should be informed by  the science but governments should not  be led by the science and if they claim  that they are led by the science what  they're trying to do  is to hide their own political choices  and therefore their  political accountability uh behind the  science and then of course what comes  next when things have gone wrong  is to blame the science um for the  political choices that they  made and i see signs of that happening  in the uk  well thank you so much steve for sharing  your insights with us  uh we've got just i think a little under  10 minutes left so i'm going to turn to  some of the questions that have come in  from our audience  um so one question  uh is that so the person has written in  that they find one of the most  challenging parts of covert 19 is that  the effects are delayed  that there's a two-week delay period  between infection and symptoms and it's  hard to know which  types of personal protective measures  are effective  um how is human psychology adapted to  dealing  with this kind of delay between actions  and consequences  yeah well i'm first of all of course the  the asymptomatic transmission issue  we've discovered  is is is is one of the most significant  uh issues of coverage and it's what  makes it actually such a  such a dangerous disease because a lot  of people who don't know that they are  ill  um are going out and and and shedding  the virus  um that's one of the reasons i mean  people thought the major parameters were  if you like how infectious it was  and how many people it killed but the  delay is critical to the epidemiology  it's also critical to issues like why we  should wear masks because a lot of the  time  we can spread the disease without  knowing that we are spreading  uh the disease  i mean there is a lot of research in  psychology  about delayed gratification and uh about  temporality and so on i think also  again actually what it comes down to is  clarity about understanding  and clarity about messaging and it comes  back to actually  an even broader issue if you want to ask  how  are authorities affected and why people  comply  with authorities the literature tends to  suggest that it's not to do with  authorities being able to punish you but  you having a sense of  uh the authorities acting for you and  the authorities  uh being on your side and so work on  procedural justice says  if you want people to 