ce and ethnicity  um certainly of class certainly of  gender of generation as well  it has shown us how pernicious they are  it has shown us how  murderous they are things that people  knew before but it's become  blindingly obvious and it's also shown  as  that the response when people are angry  at those inequalities is not repression  that simply leads to social disorder  it's to acknowledge  address and to change and so i hope  that that vision both of the  inequalities and of the  inadequacy of a repressive response will  empower us to struggle for a  more equitable society the second thing  it's shown us and  in the sense that's been at the core of  what i've been saying  is it changes our understanding  of the relationship between the  individual and the collective in a quite  profound  way we've always been told the  individual  is relatively rational and the group is  bad for  us it's an idea that came out in the  period of industrialization  basically because of elite fear of the  masses what we've seen in this pandemic  is the power of the group now of course  that power can be used in bad ways and  we see that in pandemics we see  at times we see prejudice we see pogroms  in pandemics but it can be used  positively like dynamite it can be a  destructive power it can be a  constructive power  i think understanding the power of the  collective  and the fact that the collective is not  less rational the individual  is a profound change for our social  sciences  and it raises questions of course about  the relationship between the individual  and the state and perhaps the state  needs to abandon  its paternalistic notion that people are  irrational and needs the state to look  after them  and change it to if you like a notion  that the state has to scaffold  the collective self-organization of  people it's not a cheap option  it's an option for budget cuts because  actually self-organization requires  resources  um it really it requires funds but  certainly 