  others working with others when others  become an asset  to us rather than the problem actually  we become psychologically resilient  we become empowered and we become rather  effective and what we've seen  in this pandemic in many countries  certainly we saw it in the uk i think  you saw it in  canada as well is that when that shared  sense of identity  is understood when it is nurtured by  leadership when it is scaffolded by  policies which bring people together  then  actually the public uh develop a sense  of collective resilience and that sense  of collective resilience is in many ways  the best asset you have in a pandemic  and so just to finish this comment with  for me if i had a top 10 quotes of the  pandemic  i'm sure there will be lists like that  in in sunday magazines and years to come  um my number one would be andrew cuomo  the governor of new york who said  it's not about i it's about we get your  head  around the wii concept and what my work  has tried to do for many years is to try  and understand  the we concept i call it social identity  concept will  uh do and understand how it can be  developed  and harnessed and how the group becomes  the asset rather than the problem in a  pandemic  that's interesting well i'd like to pick  up on the theme that you  mentioned around the role of political  leaders and suggesting that they have a  critical role  in fostering necessary group behaviors  and dealing with a health emergency  what do we know about what's needed in  terms of effective political leadership  in an emergency situation like pandemic  well let me start with the concept of  leadership itself  um and since since since you plugged the  the together apart book also this week  we did we uh  released with with my colleague alex  haslam and michael plathor a book on  leadership  so leadership is something that  interests me and  when you look at traditional notions of  leadership  they are again very hierarchical and  very  elitist and they start from the premise  that what makes a great leader is what  sets them apart from the rest of us you  know the great leader has these amazing  qualities  uh and if only you could understand  what it was if you could isolate this  right stuff and bottle it you would have  solved the problem of leadership  um now the problem with that well it has  many problems first of all it ignores  the fact that  leaders again don't achieve anything on  their own  they literally they achieve it through  followers so uh  leadership which ignores followers is is  an  empty model empirically i mean that  notion of of  uh it is highly gendered great man  leadership has been around for a long  time  um thomas carlisle famously in uh i  think was about 1848 his book on heroes  and hero worship said that  uh history is effectively uh  what great men have done while they were  uh  on earth um thousands of studies trying  to  find empirically what is the quality  that shows leadership  um and they've all failed and they  failed because actually they missed the  point that what makes a great leader  is what actually brings them together  with  the group they seek to lead  what makes them able to represent that  group speak  for that group shape the understanding  of the group  so what the qualities that you need in a  leader  aren't abstract or absolute it's more a  matter of a  fit between our understanding of who we  are and  the leaders definition of who they are  and leaders um we call them  entrepreneurs of identity are very  skilled at telling a narrative of the  group  and a narrative of themselves which  fleshes those two together  and particularly what you need in a  pandemic is you need a sense of  shared identity you need to nurture that  sense of  us-ness you need to bring people  together to  support each other um and actually to  to do the practical things but also to  provide the psychological support  to take us through now if one of the  criteria  for being an effective leader is to  create a unified  group then we see huge differences  between different leaders and different  places um  i think the leaders we've seen who are  effective um  uh so for instance justin ardern perhaps  in new zealand  um luckily enough i don't live in  england i live in scotland so we've seen  it with nicolas sturgeon  actually it's quite interesting when you  when you name the leaders you see they  have something in common  um they tend to be women um but these  are leaders  who have been very effective in  nurturing  uh a sense of us and in representing  that sense  of us and also of course in developing  the policies  which support the collective  self-interest  but again let me make a point here it's  a simple point but actually it's a  rather consequential point  in so much of our social science we  start from the premise  that individuals act in terms of  individual self-interest  and the way to motivate people is to  tell them what is in their individual  self-interest  the problem with that again is it  equates the self with the individual  it ignores the fact that we can define  ourselves in collective  terms and it ignores the fact that  actually in the pandemic  what is important is we don't act for  ourselves as individuals  but we act for the community so  very simply if people acting in terms of  individual self-interest the hell of a  lot of people in this pandemic  would save themselves actually if i do  the cost benefit analysis  i lose more by staying in them by going  out if you're young and you're healthy  actually you don't have much to risk uh  by going out and you lose a hell of a  lot by staying in i have a  16 year old next door who is not very  happy about having to stay in he suffers  far more than i do  so on the basis of individual  self-interest your calculation would say  go out  but of course at a collective level if  you go out  you spread the disease other people get  ill and  die so if you want to contain the  pandemic people need to be  thinking of themselves in terms of that  collective identity  and by and large that's what happened so  for instance i know  the uk data there may well be data in  canada as well but it shows  that actually the main factor which  determined whether people obeyed  lockdown or not  was not their sense of individual risk  it was their sense of communal risk  their sense of community identity so  again  understanding the we concept creating a  sense of  us and representing that sense of us is  absolutely central to leadership  however and actually here um uh i should  i i could try and show how much i know  about canada  but i know nothing about canada but  luckily yeah uh kate you you you  mean yeah i read about bonnie henry  and i loved her quotation about um we're  all in the same  uh storm we're not in the same boat and  the problem is it's all very well  to talk the talk of shared identity  and to talk the talk of an inclusive  sense of us-ness  but of course different people within  the national community are in very  different positions  and are affected very differently  um by the pandemic it's opened up and  shown a spot by tomorrow  all that all our inequalities um and  therefore  part of creating a shared identity is  not only to do it rhetorically but to do  it practically the practical economic  measures of for instance  supporting people so they could afford  to stay at home  giving uh money for those who are  unemployed giving  uh money for those who are self-employed  and and so on and only if in practice  you create the conditions for a shared  experience and a  shared response will you be able to  create that shared identity so as i say  it's a matter  of rhetoric but it's also a matter of  practical policy  uh which means that we're not only in  the same storm but we are  at least to some extent in the same boat  well now that it's been almost four  months at least  in canada since the major covet 19  restrictions were put in place  we're hearing more and more um reference  to things like quarantine fatigue  is there evidence that there are  potential limits to our psychological  ability to put up with restrictions  you see this was a huge controversy in  the uk and before we locked down  we had people talking about behavioral  fatigue uh  and they they actually used it to uh  justify  a delay uh in lockdown in the uk and  that  arguably that could have cost tens of  thousands of lives i mean  um uh depending on how you calculate it  i mean you've lost about eight thousand  lives from what i can see in canada  we've lost about sixty thousand we've  done pretty badly  um and this behave this notion of  behavioral fatigue if  it um uh led to a delay it  it's a big issue okay now  when you look at the notion of fatigue  it is perfectly  true that uh it is difficult to sustain  lockdown over a prolonged period so the  fact that there is fatigue and  compliance  decreases i think is not controversial  what is controversial is what is the  root of that fatigue  is it an essential psychological  fragility and that takes me back to  what i was saying about different  psychological models or is it something  else  so let me give you just one i think very  striking piece of evidence again i  apologize  it's british evidence but i suspect it's  the same in different places  in the uk you found a difference of a  factor of  six between the most affluent and the  least affluent in how often they went  out under under lockdown  okay there's a huge difference in  compliance  but when you looked at motivation when  you looked at the psychological will  to stay in there was no difference  between those groups at all  the difference lay in just basically the  ability to stay at home  and um uh put food on the table  so there are a couple of factors which  are absolutely critical  in determining your ability to go along  with something  one is of course the information you're  given  people might suffer for a cause but if  there's no cause to suffer for people  aren't going to suffer for it so  if the messaging is bad and if the  messaging suggests to you oh well  actually it's all over  there's no danger then people won't  comply so  failures of government messaging we've  seen that in the uk  undermine compliance and the second  thing is practical if people can't  afford  um to stay at home and feed their  families they won't do so  so to my mind the evidence for the  psychological fragility is actually  rather weak people showed  incredible resilience under lockdown i  mean  again in the uk the figures showed that  something like 44  of people were suffering but they still  stayed in i think this has  undermined those notions of fragility  and showed  collective resilience when people work  together the problems as i say have been  those of  messaging and our government has been  very bad at this  and of giving practical support and so  to talk about  fatigue being a psychological problem in  many ways for me  is the government blaming its own  failures on the failure of human  psychology  and i think that's a very dangerous uh  as well as a very unfair thing to do  so as i mentioned at the outset you've  been advising  both the scottish and uk governments on  how to encourage adherence to public  health behaviors  in a previous c4 webinar we had one of  our other fellows tim besley who's part  of our institutions organizations and  growth program who spoke  to the government use of research  evidence for policy making  this seems to be a particularly  political issue  in the uk right now can you tell us  why and how you feel government should  use insights from  academic research both to guide the  pandemic response but also  a post-pandemic recovery so i think the  responsibility  responsibility is always round and  i mean academics have got to be open  about the limits of what we know and the  certainties of our own  evidence and clearly given that  uh covert 19 is new and we're learning  things about it  all the time uh both in epidemiological  terms but also in psychological terms  there are certain things we couldn't  know yet we have some general principles  that we could apply um and there i think  one of the benefits of  of psychological research is that on the  whole we do try and understand  if you like general processes of  behavior so i can  speak for instance to the role of  social identities and social norms in  behavior  and then i can apply that to the  particular types  of identities you've drawn or the  particular types of norms  which is necessary to reconfigure in  order to get behavior so i think  i think we have to be as i say open  about our own limitations  and then from the perspective of  government i think there are two  pitfalls  one being to ignore the science  because the science of course can tell  you what the consequence of particular  actions will be  but the other error and i think it's  equally problematic  is to claim to be led by the science  because if the science can tell you  what particular outcomes are likely to  be what it can't do  is tell you you know how much risk  should you or shouldn't you take  or how do you balance different risks  against each other  because that's a political question or a  value question  if you like and to claim that you are  led by the science can sometimes  hide the political choices which i think  is dangerous and disingenuous  so i think it's very important to get  the balance right and to say that  government should listen to us  and governments should be informed by  the science but governments should not  be led by the science and if they claim  that they are led by the science what  they're trying to do  is to hide their own political choices  and therefore their  political accountability uh behind the  science and then of course what comes  next when things have gone wrong  is to blame the science um for the  political choices that they  made and i see signs of that happening  in the uk  well thank you so much steve for sharing  your insights with us  uh we've got just i think a little under  10 minutes left so i'm going to turn to  some of the questions that have come in  from our audience  um so one question  uh is that so the person has written in  that they find one of the most  challenging parts of covert 19 is that  the effects are delayed  that there's a two-week delay period  between infection and symptoms and it's  hard to know which  types of personal protective measures  are effective  um how is human psychology adapted to  dealing  with this kind of delay between actions  and consequences  yeah well i'm first of all of course the  the asymptomatic transmission issue  we've discovered  is is is is one of the most significant  uh issues of coverage and it's what  makes it actually such a  such a dangerous disease because a lot  of people who don't know that they are  ill  um are going out and and and shedding  the virus  um that's one of the reasons i mean  people thought the major parameters were  if you like how infectious it was  and how many people it killed but the  delay is critical to the epidemiology  it's also critical to issues like why we  should wear masks because a lot of the  time  we can spread the disease without  knowing that we are spreading  uh the disease  i mean there is a lot of research in  psychology  about delayed gratification and uh about  temporality and so on i think also  again actually what it comes down to is  clarity about understanding  and clarity about messaging and it comes  back to actually  an even broader issue if you want to ask  how  are authorities affected and why people  comply  with authorities the literature tends to  suggest that it's not to do with  authorities being able to punish you but  you having a sense of  uh the authorities acting for you and  the authorities  uh being on your side and so work on  procedural justice says  if you want people to go along with  authorities  in many ways treat them as you would a  member of your own group  treat them as a partner and part of  doing that  means being open with people it means  respecting people  it means giving people all the  information that they need  and one of the dangers of this fragility  notion is that if you give people too  much information  they'll panic so we will hide the  complexities from people  because they're not up to it my sense is  that people are fully  able to understand these things as long  as a  government has an adult conversation  with people  respects people and provides a clear  mental model of the nature of the  disease  and so to me this is very much not an  issue of  psychological limitations it's an issue  of the problems of the psychological  government's  uh models which authority has and it's  an issue of  clarity of communication giving us a  clear  mental model and part of that mental  model is an understanding  that we've got to act as if we've got  the virus  even when we haven't and we've got to  take the precautions  as if we could have uh the virus even  when we don't and so i think those forms  of communication  become an absolutely central issue  do you think there'll be a lasting  effect on social interactions even after  the vaccines  developed and deployed see the paradox  of that question i think is i mean so  there's all this debate  about you know how will society change  well the problem is that if you think  that societies are going to inevitably  change  in particular ways you don't fight for  those changes you become  passive which means that they're less  likely to happen  so that's the paradox peridot is the  more you say this is going to happen  the less likely it is to happen for me  i look at it slightly differently i say  what what has this  um uh pandemic shown us and i think it's  shown as two very clear things first of  all  of course it's shown a spotlight on  inequalities  of um certainly of race and ethnicity  um certainly of class certainly of  gender of generation as well  it has shown us how pernicious they are  it has shown us how  murderous they are things that people  knew before but it's become  blindingly obvious and it's also shown  as  that the response when people are angry  at those inequalities is not repression  that simply leads to social disorder  it's to acknowledge  address and to change and so i hope  that that vision both of the  inequalities and of the  inadequacy of a repressive response will  empower us to struggle for a  more equitable society the second thing  it's shown us and  in the sense that's been at the core of  what i've been saying  is it changes our understanding  of the relationship between the  individual and the collective in a quite  profound  way we've always been told the  individual  is relatively rational and the group is  bad for  us it's an idea that came out in the  period of industrialization  basically because of elite fear of the  masses what we've seen in this pandemic  is the power of the group now of course  that power can be used in bad ways and  we see that in pandemics we see  at times we see prejudice we see pogroms  in pandemics but it can be used  positively like dynamite it can be a  destructive power it can be a  constructive power  i think understanding the power of the  collective  and the fact that the collective is not  less rational the individual  is a profound change for our social  sciences  and it raises questions of course about  the relationship between the individual  and the state and perhaps the state  needs to abandon  its paternalistic notion that people are  irrational and needs the state to look  after them  and change it to if you like a notion  that the state has to scaffold  the collective self-organization of  people it's not a cheap option  it's an option for budget cuts because  actually self-organization requires  resources  um it really it requires funds but  certainly i think there are some  profound  visions we've had but they are visions  that give us a notion of what we want to  get they're not going to happen  automatically  and the things are going to have to be  fought for  um i'm looking at time and i'm trying to  decide if i have if i can squeeze in one  more question  um it relates to the fact that uh we  often we probably  well we all likely have multiple  overlapping  kinds of group identity um and one of  which may be  um affiliation with different political  parties  how particularly in canada the us and  the uk political party  affiliation can be quite a strong  element of identity  are there opportunities here to  strengthen solidarity or  to to improve adherence to  public health measures along political  party lines  i mean i hear again i think leadership  is absolutely critical i mean you can  have  polarizing leadership and in fact i mean  to me what's happened in the united  states is quite extraordinary i mean one  has a president  who uh positions himself as the  anti-political  uh president and who is an uh  you know as an insurgent even in office  the office of the president of the  united states  acts as an insurgent and encourages  people to act in  anti-state ways encourages people to  to violate lockdown encourages people to  protest and even be violent against  those who are taking public health  formations  you can have a deeply polarizing uh  leadership i think you've seen it in  other countries as well  so i think for instance uh in in india  we saw it to some extent and the ways  in which it was used to pursue a hindu  nationalist agenda  and to talk for instance about corona  jihadism to  to blame muslims so i think um  bad leadership in a pandemic is really  the worst of all  worlds and can divide people  but where there has been good inclusive  leadership as i say which goes along  with the measures that bring people  together  then yes i think we've seen  extraordinary levels of uh  of um of of unity and extraordinary  levels of  support so again we sometimes think of  contexts automatically changing human  behavior  no it's the way in which contexts are  understood  and made sense of it's the interaction  between the structure of experience and  leadership and the structure of rhetoric  which determines  outcomes it's not inevitable it's not  deterministic  it's the intersection of the political  and the psychological  which answers those questions well  uh that is uh all the time that we have  for today  unfortunately uh thank you to all who  are joining us from home and a huge  thank you to you  uh steven reicher for sharing your  insights with us  please visit our website at cfair.ca to  find out more about this  these series and to view a recording of  this talk which should be posted early  next week  could i make one final final point if i  may which is that the book  which we wrote we we  we wrote in order to be as uh broadly  available as possible so it is  available as a free download it doesn't  cost you anything you can buy it as a  paperback  but it's a free download so if you go to  look up together apart that's the name  of the book  um you can have it for free and you can  see some of the literature and the  evidence that lies behind some of the  things i've been saying  wonderful thanks so much steve thank you  you


